Is it Jess or Jessie from North Carolina?

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:09.220
It's kind of cut off.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.040
Speaker 3: Josiah as a matter of fact, josiah.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.090
Speaker 1: Welcome, josiah.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.700
Speaker 3: I appreciate it.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.700
David.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.990
Hey, I got a question, especially in light
of the last night's debate between Oz and

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:21.990
Fetterman.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:22.990
Yes.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:23.990
Speaker 1: Right.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.230
Speaker 3: Or was it not last night?

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.420
Speaker 1: Well, you know, it's Friday.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.420
Today.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.420
It's Friday today.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.500
Speaker 3: True.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.500
Touché.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.770
I got thinking about, you know, we're seeing
obviously the the polls change to the detriment

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.770
of Democrats.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.989
You've been commenting on this on your shows.

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:46.120
And I keep going back to the double standards
to which I feel that Democrats are held to,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.160
not just by conservatives, but also by centrist
moderates and people on the left.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.160
Yeah.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.289
In terms of performance and substance.

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:59.370
And I was on Twitter and I was seeing like
all the headlines from people who would be

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.810
considered, quote unquote, liberal or mainstream
journalists, which are perceived to have a

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:06.820
liberal bias.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:12.290
And they were ravaging Fetterman for his performance,
even though, you know, his communication issues,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.740
as far as we know, are just it's temporary
aphasia.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.550
It's a stroke induced speech impediment.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:21.570
Speaker 1: Yeah, I don't know how temporary
it is, to be totally honest, but it's not

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:22.570
cognitive.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:23.619
I mean, he remembered all the facts.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:25.040
He remembered all the points.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.320
He understood his position and those of Mehmet
Oz.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.549
I don't know if it's temporary, but it's certainly
it's only a speech thing.

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:33.619
That's absolutely the case.

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:34.860
Speaker 3: And fair enough.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.240
It may it may very well not be transient.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.170
But I got thinking about it and I'm like,
the GOP has been fielding and successfully

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:47.640
electing terrible communicators who, as far
as we know, don't have a medical basis like

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.030
W Bush and Palin and Trump and Gosar and GREENE.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:57.070
Why is it that everybody, not just conservatives,
but everybody, seems to hold Democrats to

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:03.240
a higher standard of performance and style
and substance in terms of politicking and

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.260
messaging and debate?

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:11.970
And is there anything that we can do as people
who want Democrats to take power to stop contributing

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.970
to that double standard?

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.740
Speaker 1: I don't know what we can do to
stop contributing to it.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.210
You know, you're asking me why?

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.210
Because they're hypocrites, right?

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.810
I don't know how to answer it any more clearly.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.700
You know, Herschel Walker, as far as we know,
didn't have a stroke.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:34.930
It's not been acknowledged that he has CTE
from playing football and he makes less sense

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:35.950
than Fetterman.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:36.950
Right.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:44.099
Fetterman had a speech issue, but he at least
communicated policy and understands the issues

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:48.480
and stuck to the issue that was being discussed,
etc., etc..

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.720
They say Fetterman should have dropped out.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.230
Bah bah bah bah bah.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.680
And they say any criticism of Herschel Walker
is racism, for example, or something like

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:56.790
that.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.410
I don't know what we can do to stop them being
hypocrites, but that's fundamentally what

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:03.410
this is.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:04.879
Speaker 3: Do you agree, though?

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:08.450
Because I guess I don't see it just as an
us versus them.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:14.390
I feel that that people who would nominally
be considered, quote unquote liberal are quote

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:20.220
unquote, you know, like like there's the perception
that the mainstream media that The New York

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.110
Times, that CNN has a liberal bias.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:28.209
And I probably agree that most New York Times
and CNN journalists, I would expect, would

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:35.769
vote Democrat, but they, I feel, hold Democrats
to higher performative and substantive standards

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:37.129
than Republicans as well.

00:03:37.129 --> 00:03:42.870
Because, again, if if the metric is this person's
a poor communicator, therefore they should

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.530
drop out of the race, then who on earth could
the Republican Party ever field?

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:48.530
Yeah.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.530
W Bush.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:50.530
Palin.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.530
Trump.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.530
So?

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:53.530
So, do you agree?

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.799
I guess you agree that it's not just conservatives
holding Democrats to a higher standard of

00:03:55.799 --> 00:03:56.799
ideology?

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:57.799
Speaker 1: I do agree.

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:02.519
And part of it is, I mean, it's a good thing
that we have higher standards, right?

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:06.640
Trump would never have won the Democratic
primary in 2016.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.500
He won the Republican primary because we have
higher, higher standards than they do.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.500
And that's a good thing.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.130
In a lot of cases, it starts to become not
so good if it contributes to more of these

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.260
right wing lunatics getting elected.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.850
Speaker 3: I agree.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:21.850
Okay.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:22.850
Well, I appreciate it.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:23.850
I'm glad.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.840
At least we were there's some sort of consensus
and I appreciate your coverage on all this.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:27.840
Keep going.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:28.840
Speaker 1: Thank you so much.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:29.840
Very much.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:30.840
Appreciate the call.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.170
Let's go to Dylan from Tijuana.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:34.639
Dylan, welcome.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:39.300
Speaker 3: When I was there, David got with
us.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.300
The Minister.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:44.450
Speaker 1: Can I tell you something that drives
me nuts about how Americans pronounce the

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.389
name of the of the city or in.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:47.900
Speaker 3: Oh, my God.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.900
Me too.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:49.900
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:56.720
They say, oh, you know, I went from San Diego
to Tijuana and I'm like, Oh, first of all.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:57.720
Speaker 3: No.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.720
Where are you?

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.330
Speaker 1: Where are you getting some of these
additional syllables?

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.270
It doesn't make any sense, Tia.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.970
Like the words for aunt, it's Tijuana.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.699
That's it, guys, please.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:09.720
Speaker 3: Yeah, I.
Speaker 1: Know.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.310
All right, Dylan, go ahead, please.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:13.310
I'm sorry.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.470
Speaker 3: Bye.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.639
My question for you is, how screwed do you
think?

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:24.630
Do you think people are in the Colorado River
basin like L.A., Las Vegas, Phenix, San Diego

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:28.000
and Tijuana for water scarcity in the future?

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.990
Speaker 1: Oh.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.420
Listen, here's the thing about it.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.720
I don't want to.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:34.720
Okay, let's see.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:35.720
How can I.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:36.720
How can I back this up?

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.560
One of the things that sometimes happens in
any kind of climate change discussion is people

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.160
will start to say, hey, listen, I agree there's
a problem.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.470
I agree if nothing changes, it'll be a disaster.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:55.819
But I'm confident that engineering and ingenuity
will figure out a solution before it really

00:05:55.819 --> 00:05:57.160
becomes a problem.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.690
And I think that that's very, very dangerous
thinking because we should be trying to figure

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.750
things out, but we should also be changing
our behavior and our consumption and all these

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.220
different things.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.200
On the issue of water.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:15.139
I actually am more confident that science
and technology is going to figure out a solution

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:17.110
in that part of the country.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:22.850
But I still think that water usage and water
rights, which are a really controversial thing

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.720
in California and in that area, need to be
kind of rethought from the ground up anyway,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:33.630
even though, based on what I'm reading about
water technology, it's probably all going

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:34.840
to work out okay.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:42.789
Although there may be irreversible changes
to the fertility of certain parts of those

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:46.730
states as climate changes and water becomes
more scarce.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:50.810
Not drinking water, but but sort of groundwater
is, as the case may be.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.800
So that's that's a really complicated issue.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.220
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:01.569
And here in Tijuana with it's happened a couple
of times this year and several times last

00:07:01.569 --> 00:07:07.070
year where we'll have tens of thousands of
people without water because they're making

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:13.349
repairs to the to the water lines, supposedly
for, you know, better efficiency and better

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:19.080
management, stuff like that, that I go out
and drive in the streets of Tijuana and there's

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:24.419
just these large leaks, just, you know, roads
are wet and it's a rainy day because.

00:07:24.419 --> 00:07:25.560
Yeah.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:31.000
So I as a consumer in general, on these climate
change things, I'm tired of being blamed for

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:36.770
my individual choices when it's the large
government authorities and large industries

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.720
that are really, really messing up.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.790
Speaker 1: It's like the thing about straws,
you know, and it's like, wait, hold on a second,

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:43.810
let's back up a little bit.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.710
Or, you know, I took a seven instead of a
five minute shower.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:54.520
But look at the almond and avocado growers
and how they're, you know, using ungodly amounts

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:55.520
of water.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.130
Yeah, no, I totally know what you mean.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:58.440
The drops in the bucket.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.420
Speaker 3: Exactly right.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.870
Well, I really appreciate you coming down
coming down to our side of the border.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.690
And if you're ever in Tijuana, hit me up.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:07.690
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:10.240
So, like, what are the what are the best tacos
in Tijuana?

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.190
Speaker 3: The tacos you're eating at the
time.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:14.190
Oh, okay.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.190
Speaker 1: They're all.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:16.190
They're all good.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:18.220
Speaker 3: Yeah, I will say one thing.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:25.770
In Tijuana, we have a slight regional variation
on some other border.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:29.810
Alabama in Tijuana mean something different
from the rest of the country.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.659
The rest of the country and the U.S. will
call it all bastards.

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:36.399
But we have our own little kind of twist,
regional twist on it.

00:08:36.399 --> 00:08:37.760
So I highly recommend Alabama.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.930
Speaker 1: That's that's a fair recommendation.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:43.789
Have you had Americans go down and ask about
tacos?

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.920
Have you heard that pronunciation before?

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.610
Speaker 3: Are you just like asking the credits,
don't you, Dave?

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:51.610
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:52.610
I've heard everything.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:53.610
Yeah.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:54.610
Speaker 1: Yeah, no, I.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:58.010
The other day, a friend of mine said tacos,
and I was like, What is happening?

00:08:58.010 --> 00:08:59.010
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:00.070
Speaker 1: Anyway, all right.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:01.070
